Module five, video 5.8, watch the bus: monitoring and anomaly detection

2
00:00:06,580 --> 00:00:14,100
Point Claude at the bus's operational monitoring data, never its message log, to spot a failing or unusual exchange early.

3
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:15,460
So, uh,

4
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:18,760
looking at this from a director general's perspective,

5
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:23,380
what exactly happens to the infrastructure after the bus is actually running?

6
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:28,280
Well, a running bus is-- I mean, it is not finished, right?

7
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:33,460
It operates every day. Right. And every single call leaves a record in the operational monitoring data.

8
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:35,560
Oh, like what kind of record?

9
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:41,500
Uh, things like which agency called, you know, if it actually worked, and how long the whole thing took.

10
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:42,700
Okay, got it.

11
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:46,540
But reading all of that by hand just, well, it does not scale at all.

12
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,480
So the AI play here is to turn this data into a plain language health picture, you know, and flag issues automatically.

13
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:59,900
That makes a lot of sense, but what exactly does this monitoring data reveal about the services in your program?

14
00:01:00,540 --> 00:01:02,260
Yeah, so it basically shows three things.

15
00:01:02,860 --> 00:01:06,740
Uh, number one is which exchanges failed and, you know, how often they failed.

16
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:08,520
Okay, so you are tracking the errors.

17
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:16,200
Exactly. Number two is latency. So if calls are getting slower, that usually indicates a service is, well, under strain.

18
00:01:16,660 --> 00:01:18,260
Oh, right, like a bottleneck or something.

19
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:21,620
Yeah, precisely. And then number three is unusual patterns.

20
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:23,240
Like what kind of patterns?

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:31,380
I mean things like sudden spikes in traffic, uh, new callers out of nowhere, or surges at like three AM.

22
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:34,160
No. Yeah, you definitely wanna catch a three AM surge.

23
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:36,760
Right, because this provides early warnings.

24
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:40,040
Mm-hmm. So a failing service is fixed before it ever becomes a complaint.

25
00:01:40,460 --> 00:01:42,000
That is huge for a ministry.

26
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:45,720
It really is. And there is also a quarterly loop.

27
00:01:46,060 --> 00:01:52,800
The operating authority tells the steering committee which members show sustained failures, uh, requiring a conformance recheck.

28
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:56,460
Okay, so when you are implementing this oversight for your minister,

29
00:01:57,160 --> 00:02:00,320
what are the actual rules or, you know, safeguards for this whole process?

30
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:03,540
Well, there are two non-optional safeguards you need to follow.

31
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:07,140
Number one, the AI flags, but a human investigates.

32
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:10,199
Oh, so it is not just fully automated action.

33
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:14,340
No, not at all. An anomaly is just a question to look into.

34
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:17,040
I mean, it is not a verdict to act on automatically.

35
00:02:17,260 --> 00:02:19,760
That makes total sense. And the second safeguard?

36
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:24,060
Number two is monitor the traffic, never the cargo.

37
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:26,200
Wait, traffic versus cargo?

38
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:30,620
Yeah. You explicitly distinguish the two records within the reference architecture.

39
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:34,860
The operational monitoring data, which is the traffic, goes into the prompt.

40
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:40,540
Right. But the message log, which is the cargo containing the actual citizen data, well, that never goes in.

41
00:02:40,980 --> 00:02:43,680
Oh, wow. Yeah, that is a critical distinction to make.

42
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:48,880
Exactly. You must check request addresses to remove ID numbers before data even leaves the server.

43
00:02:49,620 --> 00:02:55,560
And, uh, a hard rule is never use the phrase the logs without specifying which record you mean.

44
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:58,320
So precision is really everything here.

45
00:02:58,660 --> 00:03:01,900
It is. Monitoring keeps a framework healthy as it grows.

46
00:03:02,380 --> 00:03:11,140
The bus records in its monitoring data, the AI play makes that legible, the operating authority acts on the flags, and the messages themselves never enter the prompt.

47
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:17,040
It is the difference between a federation someone is watching and one that fails silently until a citizen complains.

